---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const varianceExample = `variances:
  src/parser/grammar.ts:
    kind: architectural
    reason: >
      Grammar definitions are intentionally centralized and easier to
      understand in a single file.
    metrics:
      scc.code_lines:
        max: 1274
      scc.complexity:
        max: 120
        reason: >
          Complexity is primarily driven by parser state generation.`;

const varianceCommand = `considered variance add \\
  --subject src/parser/grammar.ts \\
  --metric scc.code_lines \\
  --kind architectural \\
  --reason "Grammar definitions are intentionally centralized."`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Variances"
  description="Document intentional departures from repository standards."
  current="/variances/"
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1>Variances</h1>
    <p>
      Variances are not suppressions. They are reviewed departures from a
      standard, attached to exact subjects with required rationale. When a value
      falls outside its standard but stays inside its approved boundary, the report
      lists it as a passing variance. If it falls outside that boundary too, the report
      lists a violation with the variance's rationale. Values that return within
      the original standard no longer appear as variances.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="yaml" code={varianceExample} />

    <h2>Exact subjects</h2>
    <p>
      Variances target exact repository-relative paths, not glob patterns, so
      each departure maps to one reviewed file.
    </p>

    <h2>Per-metric reasons</h2>
    <p>
      A variance has an overall reason, and individual metric overrides can add
      a more specific reason when the general rationale is not enough.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding from the CLI</h2>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={varianceCommand} />

    <p>
      Adding a variance for a value that already satisfies its standard is
      rejected. Excluded subjects are also rejected. Omit the subject, metric,
      kind, or reason to enter it interactively; <code>--metric-reason</code> is
      optional. Adding another metric to the same subject preserves its other
      metric overrides, but can replace the subject's overall kind and reason.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
